% We test whether the Necessity-Plasticity decomposition extends beyond the two task-sensitive estimators by projecting every other available layer-importance estimator onto the $(\rho_P, \rho_N)$ plane, where $\rho_P$ is the mean Spearman correlation between the estimator's per-layer scores and LoRA-delta scores (Plasticity loading), and $\rho_N$ is the same versus ablation (Necessity loading). Figure~\ref{fig:decomposition-validation} shows the result. On transformers, four of five estimators load clearly on one side: \emph{Gradient} ($\rho_P{=}-0.66$, $\rho_N{=}+0.47$) and \emph{Resnorm} ($\rho_P{=}-0.89$, $\rho_N{=}+0.46$) project onto Necessity; \emph{Activation norm} ($\rho_P{=}+0.84$, $\rho_N{=}-0.35$) and \emph{TELL-TALE} ($\rho_P{=}+0.65$, $\rho_N{=}-0.32$) project onto Plasticity; \emph{ShapLoRA} sits near the boundary ($\rho_P{=}+0.22$, $\rho_N{=}-0.08$). The classification has a mechanistic reading: estimators evaluated at the pretrained checkpoint (Gradient, Resnorm) load on Necessity, while estimators that incorporate fine-tuning trajectories or post-adaptation activations (Act-norm, TELL-TALE) load on Plasticity. On Mamba, where Necessity and Plasticity are aligned at the family level, all tested estimators land in the mixed-positive region. No estimator falls in the lower-left (anti-correlated with both), so the two anchors span the relevant region of the plane.


